WEBVTT

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:07.250
 You know, we were the first certified organic dairy and creamery in the western United States in the beginning of 1994.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:14.930
 Now, probably close to 90% of the dairies in Marin County are certified organic, and actually 80 to 90% in Sonoma County as well.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:22.530
 And we haven't lost any dairies. Looking at a trend, well, in the United States, there were 4.6 million dairy farms in 1940.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:30.820
 Now there's 43,000 left today. I think going organic and starting the creamery were all the same decision.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:36.260
 We had actually done organic vegetables, and we hadn't used any herbicides since the early 70s.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.460
 We hadn't used any synthetic fertilizers since the early 80s.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:45.460
 In my mind, organic is where we wanted to get to. We wanted to make a sustainable system.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:52.180
 We're still working on how can we make farms sustainable, how can we make them where they're a positive influence

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:59.610
 and a positive food supply for the community, and make a model that can be replicated other places.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.930
 We don't sell nationally. We don't sell internationally.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:08.060
 The idea is to build a regional model that other people can replicate around the country and around the world.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.980
 Well, I think there's huge challenges to the next generation.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.120
 How can you start a farm when you can't get land, you can't get housing, you can't get financing?

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.790
 So that's another aspect of food movement is how do you move it on to the next generation.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:29.330
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:30.330
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:36.450
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.730
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:46.410
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.390
 kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.230
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:59.830
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:10.670
 Albert Strauss is the founder and CEO of Strauss Family Creamery and an advocate for organic, non-GMO dairy production, environmental stewardship, and family farms.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:16.270
 He grew up on his family's dairy on the coast of Marin County, California, near Point Reyes National Park.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:23.550
 Strauss continues to be a leader in sustainability with projects that include independent verification that his feed is GMO-free,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.990
 a methane digester, and a water reuse system that recycles wastewater from the creamery.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.910
 His carbon farming program is a model for the Marin Carbon Project and the California Healthy Soils Initiative.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:44.300
 Thanks so much for being here and for making some time to talk to us on Delicious Revolution.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.840
 Thanks for being here.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:54.680
 I do want to start in the beginning because you grew up on the dairy ranch out on Tomales Bay.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.320
 And what did it look like there?

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.740
 What was life like there at that time?

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.380
 Well, I grew up on our dairy farm in Marshall.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.720
 And my father had started the farm in 1941.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.340
 And ran it as a conventional farm.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.620
 It was a family farm that started with 23 cows.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:24.100
 We had a partner for 20 years that they did a lot of the work together.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:33.460
 But it was always a struggle to make it profitable, make it something that you really could sustain as a family farm.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.990
 And so there was always this pressure to be able to sell out at the end.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.510
 If everything went, if you couldn't make it in the farming business, you would sell out for development.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.570
 And so that was something that my parents didn't agree with.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:04:07.750
 They worked very hard to start environmental organizations, community organizations, trying to facilitate between environmentalists, farmers, and governmental agencies to try to see how we could preserve the land in Marin County and keep it in agriculture and keep it open.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:18.700
 And so there was a very, it took decades, but there was a very good synergy and kind of collaboration between all the different groups.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:28.560
 And they passed, well, the Board of Supervisors passed one house per 60 acre zoning, agricultural zoning in the 70s.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:43.130
 And actually, let me back up a little bit, because in the 60s, there was actually a plan to put 100,000 people in West Marin on a four-lane freeway and in one house per acre, even more dense than that.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.910
 And so this one house per 60 acre zoning, agricultural zoning, was a huge revolutionary thing.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.850
 But it could be overturned on a majority of the Board of Supervisors.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:05:03.010
 So my mother and her friend, Phyllis Faber, got together and came up with the idea of starting a land trust, an agricultural land trust.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.530
 And it was the first agricultural land trust in the nation.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:19.890
 But the idea was that they would pay farmers to take the potential for development off their land and keep it in trust and keep it in agriculture in perpetuity and be compensated for that.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:28.150
 And so that was something that I was kind of born in this environment of environmentalism, farming, family farming.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.010
 We had done organic.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:40.020
 Well, actually, in high school, I started, I think around the first Earth Day, I started a recycling club at the high school.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:46.390
 And it was, you know, it was something different.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:54.530
 But we kind of looked at, and actually one of the projects I did was on the ecology of West Marin for school.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:08.240
 But it was born out of my parents' vision and life work to preserve the land and the community in West Marin.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:15.720
 West Marin's a really incredible place for, I mean, we are focusing on California in this season, but we do have a global audience.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.350
 It feels really wild, and it's really close to urban areas.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.630
 Does it look and feel a lot like when you were growing up still?

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:37.610
 We've, well, the communities, we've lost our community, and we're losing, we've been able to stabilize agriculture, but we've got a lot of forces against us right now.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:42.020
 And actually, some things I lose sleep about.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:46.920
 But in Marshall, the community we have, there's, I think, somewhere around 125 homes.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.320
 Less than 30% of them are actually lived in because it's all short-term rentals.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.940
 It's, you know, people can't afford to live and work in the community because they're now businesses.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:10.420
 We came up with a community plan back in the early 80s that tried to keep a balance of, you know, tourism and ag culture and mariculture and just a working community.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:12.740
 And we've had that erode.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:22.410
 I think ag culture, we have actually, you know, we were the first certified organic dairy and creamery in the western United States, west of Mississippi River, and began in 1994.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:31.260
 Now, it's probably close to 90% of the dairies in Marin County are certified organic, and actually 80 to 90% in Sonoma County as well.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:36.530
 And we haven't, we haven't lost any dairies, which is looking at a trend.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.710
 Well, in the United States, there were 4.6 million dairy farms in 1940.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.170
 Now there's 43,000 left today.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:49.690
 And in Marin County, I think there was 150 in early 60s, and now there are 25 left.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:08:06.330
 And so it's this trend, how do you stabilize a farming community that has these, you know, conventional dairy, you have a big volatility in pricing month to month, so you can't plan or budget or reinvest in your infrastructure or pass it on to the next generation.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:12.760
 Who would want to, what child would want to grow up on a farm that they can't pay themselves.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:22.940
 And so we're making progress on that, and, you know, we've got huge challenges with the national park that owns a third of the land in West Marin.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:28.160
 And it was formed on the premise that part of it was both state and agriculture.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.580
 And there's six organic dairies in the National Seashore.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:42.020
 There's, I think, 20 or 25 livestock operations in the park, and they're on year-to-year leases.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.290
 They can't, you know, make improvements on land.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:50.990
 They can't, or the infrastructure can't, they have no security, can't get loans to do anything.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.450
 So it's not a sustainable way to farm.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.330
 And so that's a concern for me.

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:04.020
 You grow up on a farm out there, and at some point you do decide to take off and go to college.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:04.620
 Is that right?

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:28.000
 Yeah, I grew up on the farm, fed calves, fed chickens, did a lot of things around the farm growing up, 4-H with, you know, I taught and took electricity and doing different types of projects that I think we've lost in our school systems and stuff like that.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:36.920
 But so when I went to college, I had to kind of decide I wanted to either do electrical engineering or farming.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.580
 And I had to decide which direction to go.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.600
 And I liked farming because I could be my own boss.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.030
 I could be outdoors, which is not as much anymore.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:55.900
 But it was something that I really enjoyed, and I loved animals also.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:01.380
 So it was something that I continued on.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:06.640
 I went to Cal Poly in San Luis Obispo and major in dairy science.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:13.520
 And then my senior thesis was on setting up a processing plant, a milk processing plant.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:31.130
 And it was – I had taken – I've been on the dairy products judging team, so I won third place in the Western Regional Dairy Products Scoring Contest in ice cream.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.630
 So it was something that I really enjoyed.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.430
 I learned how to make ice cream, you know.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:38.450
 You won an award in ice cream tasting.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.970
 Ice cream, yeah, for defects, yes.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:54.620
 So it was – but I came back to the farm, became partners with my father, and kind of put out my mind that it was going to do anything about processing or anything else.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:00.310
 I just was working on how to make a conventional farm viable.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:07.230
 It was – you have 46% of your income goes to feed the animals.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.810
 So that was the biggest thing I was working on.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:17.390
 So I looked through the yellow pages and tried to find feeds or different waste products that I could use for feed.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:25.290
 I used sake waste and tofu waste and cocoa bean hulls and I think it was lettuce and cabbage.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.190
 I'm trying to think what else I used.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:38.960
 Anyway, oh, I used – I had a Nabisco contract for a couple years, too, during the 89 earthquake where the whole plant fell – the chimney fell down on the plant in Oakland.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:51.470
 I worked a lot to try to make it more economically viable, but the prices never went above minimum price.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:55.290
 And it kept – you know, it was always at a depressed price.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.930
 Dairymen will – you know, when the price is up, they produce more milk.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.090
 When the price is down, they produce more milk.

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:06.890
 So it was a kind of catch-22 where you always have a surplus of milk and the prices stay depressed.

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.070
 And so it's a big roller coaster.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.590
 And who was buying the milk at that point?

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:22.250
 We were part of – well, actually, in the late 60s, I think we were part of Challenge that was a co-op that went broke.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:30.470
 And then in the 70s, we went to – we were part of California Cooperative Creamery, which is California Gold, that went broke also.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:38.760
 But just around the time it went broke is when someone approached me about the idea of making organic milk for ice cream.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:51.990
 And it was 1990, and I kind of – they kind of gave up on the idea they were going to go to other manufacturers to look at doing that.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:55.730
 But I kept looking into what organic was.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:56.910
 It was a new law in California.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.550
 There was no federal law yet.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:18.240
 And then how to build a processing plant, how to feed – well, back on the dairy side – how to feed the animals organically, how to – you know, what it meant for the land, what it meant for – how to treat them organically without antibiotics and hormones.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:26.510
 And then also how to build a plant, get financing, equipment, everything, and develop the market.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:30.800
 So that took me three and a half years to figure out.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:41.820
 And we ended up getting started – underfunded, but getting some loans from family and friends and also leasing equipment.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:47.020
 And so we were able to get started in the beginning of 1994.

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:50.090
 Was that here in Petaluma?

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:54.660
 No, it was actually – we were still in the same place 22 years later.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:06.000
 I found an abandoned commercial kitchen in Marshall that was – used to be part of Synanon, the drug and alcohol rehab that turned into a cult.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:12.360
 And their commercial kitchen that they served 1,000 to 2,000 people in Marshall.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:13.920
 It was the national headquarters.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:24.600
 And so there was a woman who has it as a – had bought it from the San Francisco Foundation at the time.

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.360
 And it was just sitting there.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:33.480
 So I was able to get that, refurbish it, and get equipment in there.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:38.160
 Actually, we got the equipment installed like in, I think, a month and a half.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.180
 But very little.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.600
 You know, we started very little and then built it over time.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:48.780
 So what was behind that choice to start the creamery?

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:55.590
 And did it come before the idea to convert to organic, or was it part of that conversion for you?

00:14:56.040 --> 00:15:02.830
 I think going organic and starting the creamery were all the same decision.

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:12.170
 We had actually done organic vegetables, and my mother grew shiitake mushrooms for a while, and we were selling in farmer's markets.

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:21.110
 But we hadn't used any herbicides since the early 70s on our land.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.690
 We hadn't used any synthetic fertilizers since the early 80s.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:26.950
 So the land was all certifiable.

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:32.000
 In my mind, organic is where we wanted to get to.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.680
 We wanted to make a sustainable system.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:47.100
 We're still working on how can we make farms sustainable, how can we make them where they're a positive influence and a positive food supply for the community,

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:51.740
 and make a model that can be replicated other places.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:52.980
 We don't sell nationally.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:54.120
 We don't sell internationally.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.300
 There's been little odds and ends that have happened.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:03.840
 But the idea is to build a regional model that other people can replicate around the country and around the world.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.260
 So those are tied hand in hand.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:17.190
 You know, I decided to use reusable glass bottles as packaging for milk, not to homogenize the milk so it has cream coming to the top.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:24.270
 So we tried to do minimally processed, no additives, and the highest quality we can make.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:27.130
 And that is built.

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:33.790
 If we didn't have the quality, I don't think we would have consumers that would want to pay the extra cost for that.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:41.840
 So that's what we kind of try to keep true to and try to work towards.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:47.880
 You know, we first started with, you know, just milk, like whole milk and non-fat.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:55.840
 Actually, I did a survey before I started at the farmer's market and out in the public about what people drank.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:58.240
 They said I did my business plan.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:04.220
 Before I did my business or incorporated in my business plan, 80% of the people said they drank non-fat or low-fat milk.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:08.590
 So when we started, I thought I'd have all this cream to make butter.

00:17:09.580 --> 00:17:18.300
 And it turned out we had over, about between 60% and 70% of our customers brought only whole milk, the cream top whole milk.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:23.240
 And so it was like so much for my model and my business plan.

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:33.950
 But it was something that was reminiscent for a lot of people of the milk delivery, you know, the milkman and the home delivery.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:39.090
 And there wasn't too much like that around.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:41.290
 So it was a good place to start.

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:50.990
 And then I had Chez Panisse and Alice Waters come to the farm and ask about us making butter just like they make in France.

00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:52.670
 I said, what's it mean?

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:54.310
 And she said, I don't know.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.590
 Maybe it's the grass or the clover.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:02.730
 And so I started making butter and asking, you know, how it was, did it meet our expectations?

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:03.890
 And it did.

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:07.650
 When you start this conversion to organic, you start building a creamery.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:16.700
 You've written, I was reading the website, and some of your neighbors thought you were kind of crazy for doing this.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:18.320
 Is that how it was?

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:22.260
 Yes, it was definitely that neighbors thought it was crazy.

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:30.990
 But actually, a lot of them are either, either they converted to organic or they aren't in the dairy business anymore.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:41.520
 So it was something that I figured that a lot of the things I do, I do to try them out and to see if it works or doesn't.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:46.150
 And then other people hopefully will incorporate it if it works.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:47.970
 So organic was one.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:57.070
 I'm still, I think we're still the only creamery and dairy that actually tests for every load of feed for GMOs

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:02.690
 and asks for certificate analysis on every load of feed and every ingredient.

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:14.040
 I found GMOs in our certified organic corn in 2005 and then started our own verification in 2008

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:19.370
 and then became the first one, first creamery in North America to be verified non-GMO

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.470
 in the beginning of 2010 under the non-GMO project.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:33.520
 And so I think it's organic integrity is what we try to maintain and really hold tight to

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:44.090
 because if we lose customers' confidence and trust in what organic food is and the organic label,

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:49.230
 we'll end up like in the UK where they had a 10-year decline in organic sales

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:53.960
 because in my estimation, the supermarket chains took over the labels.

00:19:55.110 --> 00:20:02.290
 There was a disconnect between farming methods and quality and consumers only thought it was more expensive.

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:09.580
 So that's what, in my mind, caused the decline, which is just starting to recover from.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:14.610
 From the beginning, the idea was to make kind of a model that others could replicate

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:20.080
 because it seems like I'd see glass bottles and really good milk all over the country now, I think.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:30.110
 Yeah, and a lot of people have asked us for advice and we've tried to help other farmers

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:34.800
 and other communities be able to get organic milk and organic products.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:41.020
 So I think that the food system and the farming system that we've had for the last six years hasn't worked.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:46.770
 You know, as I said, we've lost all these farmers, you know, we've lost rural communities.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:48.850
 We need to change it.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:58.450
 And so by having a sustainable organic food system and help revitalize rural communities,

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:04.220
 that's something that I think we can bring to the table and help other communities do as well.

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:09.840
 I was asked to go by Dr. Vedana Shiva to India, I think it's about four years ago now,

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:15.800
 to go to India to help subsistent dairy farmers go towards organic practices and help on the quality of milk.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:25.120
 And so we've been working in India, just having calls to people in India to help them eliminate antibiotics,

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:31.060
 double their milk production, and working on the sanitary milking practices even by hand

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:33.640
 to get as good a quality of milk as it can here.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:40.460
 You know, there's a next step to get them double the milk, double the price for their milk from consumers

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:44.300
 so they can actually make it a primary income source.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:49.010
 Are Indian farmers facing some of those same kind of challenges that you are?

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:53.980
 Farmers all over the world are facing the same issues.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:22:00.920
 There's different scale, different issues, but I mean, not different issues.

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:02.460
 It's pretty much the same issues.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:09.250
 A lot of people don't get exposure to outside advice that actually looks at it differently.

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:12.610
 You know, they have state veterinarians that treat the cows with antibiotics.

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:14.670
 You know, there's no prevention.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:16.850
 There's no looking at it differently.

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:24.170
 And so coming from my experience as a dairyman, I can talk to farmers around the world and talk about my experiences

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:28.370
 and how I've been able to change that paradigm.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:34.330
 Do you see yourself as part of a food movement?

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:38.430
 Yeah.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:50.130
 I think we're trying to change our community, our food system, to be something that's sustainable for everybody,

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:57.630
 for, you know, farmers, for the land, for the farmers, for the employees, for the animals,

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:05.430
 and create a food system that's healthy, you know, bring something positive.

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:07.070
 So we're working on climate change.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:10.450
 You know, that's the biggest issue that we're dealing with today is, you know, through,

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:17.430
 we've had a methane digester that takes all our waste from our animals and produce all our electricity for the farm.

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:22.220
 And we get hot water from that Russian equipment.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:27.520
 And then we have electric vehicles that we run off of that as well.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:33.470
 And I'm putting together, hopefully this week I'll have a test run of our full-scale truck to feed the cows.

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:35.250
 It'll be powered by the waste from the cows.

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:40.750
 And so that's one part of it.

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:49.450
 And then the other part is we were the first dairy on the marine carbon project to look at how can you sequester carbon

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.530
 from the atmosphere back into the soil.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:56.500
 And it could be by adding compost, planting hedgerows and windbreaks,

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:05.010
 having animals graze the pastures that actually promotes more plant growth that, you know, it's a cycle.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:11.590
 So, you know, put more carbon back into the soil by adding, if you put a half an inch of compost on,

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:16.930
 the statistic is if you put a half an inch of compost on half the rangeland in California,

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:22.850
 you would offset all the commercial and residential energy uses.

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:29.030
 And then we calculated if you put methane digesters on two-thirds of dairies in California,

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.390
 you would offset the effects of a million cars.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:41.130
 And so with this new law that just passed about a month ago to reduce methane emissions by 40% by 2030,

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:48.810
 we're looking at working, we've been working with this company to build and operate methane digesters on farms

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:54.500
 and looking at how we can make it a model that farms can incorporate,

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:59.480
 our farms can incorporate and other farms where they're not actually running another business

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:07.240
 trying to, you know, produce energy and deal with this other whole operation that takes their energy away from farming.

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:12.150
 So there's a lot of different dynamics going on.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:19.290
 We're also part of Patrick Holden and the Sustainable Food Trust in the UK had a conference in April in San Francisco,

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:26.110
 the true cost of American food that we had four of our dairies do a survey by Dr.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:29.010
 Harpenter Sandhu of Australia,

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:41.950
 who's an ecosystem services professor and advisor to the UN on climate change to use metrics to look at the environmental costs and benefits

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:45.470
 and the social costs and benefits and equate it to a gallon of milk.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:53.380
 And we were in this conference with Joel Salatin was one of the people that did the evaluation

00:25:53.380 --> 00:26:00.020
 and then a corn and soybean farmer from Minnesota also was part of it.

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:07.000
 And so there was a lot of learnings around that and a lot of learnings about the effects of food,

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:09.680
 the food system on health and on the environment.

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:12.320
 I guess I asked the question about being part of the food movement.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:14.700
 I guess I am.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:16.360
 No, I mean, it makes sense to me.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:23.440
 I think Chelsea and I, one of the more kind of itches that got us to start the podcast was thinking about like,

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.380
 oh, where do we fit in a food movement?

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:27.980
 And maybe it's like a broader set of people and we thought.

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:31.980
 And I recently talked for the same season, talked to Nikki Silvestri,

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:35.900
 who's been working on the Marine Carbon Priority, doing interviews, gathering data for them.

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:37.180
 And it kind of surprised me.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:39.320
 I was like, well, where does the food movement go now?

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:40.180
 Or like, what's moving?

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:42.540
 It's like, oh, we need a lot more businesses.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:50.350
 We need to work on our economic power as a food movement as well as our lobbying power and our movement building.

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:53.530
 Well, I think there's huge challenges to the next generation.

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:58.630
 How can you start a farm when you can't get land, you can't get housing, you can't get financing?

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:05.090
 So that's another aspect of, you know, the food movement is how do you move it on to the next generation?

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:09.090
 And how do you, I think there's a lot of people cooking food.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:11.650
 You know, there's just not that many people growing food.

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:17.700
 And so I come from the farming end course, you know, so I look at it from that aspect.

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:22.540
 But it's, I think it's all of us working together because we are still a minority.

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:31.180
 You know, even though I like to say that we're now a majority, we are the norm and we're not a niche in dairy in Marinston County.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:35.960
 We're the norm and conventional is the exception, is the niche.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:42.600
 So, but it's, it's, it's, it's a, it's a huge, it's a huge issue.

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:43.760
 It's a huge challenge.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.080
 And it's, and it's going to take everybody working together.

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:55.900
 Like it did, like we've made the changes, my, my parents' generation, you know, just kind of working through the challenges of land use.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:28:05.640
 And, you know, I think we have the same challenges of, you know, rural communities and of farming that we've had, you know, for the last 60 years.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:18.500
 Let's talk about the rural communities just a little bit because I, you say you're just working on some, an internal project, talking with some workers about their experience.

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:26.320
 And I know, and you mentioned that it was, it's quite difficult to live in West Marin County.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:31.320
 And I know that from a certain, I've tried and never been able to find a place.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:49.340
 I mean, the one challenge we have is that the Coast Commission was supposed to be where they were enhancing and promote or encouraging agriculture in balance with community, in balance with tourism.

00:28:49.960 --> 00:29:00.610
 It's now swung all the way to tourism and, you know, and what I see is that we're going to not have our rural communities or our farming in the future on the coast of California.

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:08.610
 So it's a huge concern for me, you know, most of our workforce is, is our Hispanic.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:10.610
 Most of them are immigrants.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:23.910
 And, and we've actually, they had an internal project of talking, you know, putting a video presentation of people's stories of how they came to this country, which we just saw today.

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:25.570
 So it was the first part.

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:30.340
 And there's some really amazing stories.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:38.810
 But we're looking actually at moving our creamery to Santa Rosa because we're too far out in the country.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:39.750
 There's no housing.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:42.530
 There's, you know, it's, it's, it's people have to commute.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:47.760
 I think that's part of the problem is that people can't live and work in the same community.

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:54.140
 So, you know, we have worse traffic than I've ever seen in my life on the, on one-on-one corridor.

00:29:54.800 --> 00:30:02.080
 So how do you come up with solutions for, I'm not giving up on our rural communities.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:20.100
 In my mind, we need to hold everybody accountable to, to our future and to look past smaller views to, to bigger, bigger views and to the future of having, having our communities, viable communities and viable farming and a viable food system.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:24.120
 And it's going to take a lot of work to make that happen.

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:25.490
 Yeah.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:29.030
 I mean, and, and yeah, that's a great answer.

00:30:29.150 --> 00:30:43.240
 And it's, when I think about really good organic food and amazing successful projects like the Marin Agricultural Land Trust, one of the most challenging aspects of that is that it becomes kind of elite.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:43.920
 And so.

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:56.400
 I just came up with a proposal to the national park, um, um, to, um, to have Marin and cultural land trust manage all the ag land in the national parks or in the park in Marin County.

00:30:56.990 --> 00:31:10.030
 And they would start with this one 52 acre ranch in Marshall that allows four to six small scale produce or small animal farmers to live and work on this one ranch.

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:18.690
 And so there'd be, there's three houses, three houses there, there's, you know, uh, the park wants to have it in open space and, and not have anybody live there.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:28.570
 And so, um, at least that's the, that's the direction that I've seen proposed, you know, so it's the absence of people and a working environment in our parks.

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:39.040
 And I think that's, it's gone too far to one extreme when that was the whole founding principle of this national park that there was an area for wilderness.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:47.270
 There was an area for wildlife and there was an area for farms and, um, uh, we were kind of gone away from that.

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:54.240
 Chelsea had an art residency out in West Marin a few years ago, but right at the height of the oyster controversies.

00:31:54.500 --> 00:32:01.740
 And it seems like what was on everyone's mind was like, what kind of community do we want to have here on one hand, but also like what's nature for?

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:03.320
 Is it just to look at it?

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:04.040
 Is it to work in?

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:05.300
 Is there a balance?

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:06.590
 Right.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:19.430
 And what I put in, I did an op-ed piece in the local paper about the park services taken out over 130 houses in, in, in West Marin in the last, in the last 50 years.

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:29.960
 The leases are over, the houses are abandoned, and then, um, they wait 10 years and then they're too far gone before they, so they feel they have to destroy them.

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:33.860
 And I, I kind of said, you know, how can we work with the community?

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:38.670
 Can we put it into CLAM, which is the community affordable housing land trust?

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:44.360
 How can we look at it as a community rather than, you know, black and white?

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:48.050
 There's, there's grays that we're not even looking at, or shades of gray.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:56.470
 So, um, yeah, I think it's a huge, and the oyster cut was the same thing, is that it was supposed to be a non-conforming use in the park.

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:58.710
 And, um, it was supposed to be staying there.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:04.810
 And it was a family that had been there, has been there for, you know, two, two generations.

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:07.910
 And, um, I've known it very well.

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:09.990
 And so it's, it's a community member.

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:17.580
 So, you know, we need to look beyond the extremes and look towards, you know, how can we make it?

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:19.340
 And it was part of our food system as well.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:34.560
 Yeah, it was, it was easy for me to decide where to decide I came down on, um, partly because I love oysters, but partly because it's, um, yeah, that's part of what makes it so special out there is there's a, it's a working landscape, right?

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:42.800
 It's a, it's dynamic and it's the, that's the, a lot of, in a, in a community that, um, can feel like it lacks diversity.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:45.140
 A lot of the diversity comes from the folks who live there.

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:46.310
 Right.

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:52.020
 And, you know, yeah, the communities and we, we don't have enough of young people.

00:33:52.340 --> 00:33:53.600
 People that can afford to live there.

00:33:53.800 --> 00:34:21.310
 So we, you know, literally people are dying off, you know, so it's, um, um, and it's, it's, uh, discouraging, but I think we can also keep, continue to work to, to, to make an, you know, equitable environment for, so, so people can come to our community and live there and, and hopefully farm or, or do support type of businesses that, you know, revitalize our community.

00:34:22.130 --> 00:34:22.570
 Right.

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:45.170
 Um, I wanted to ask also about, um, about carbon farming and about climate change, another, another huge topic to talk about, but, um, um, I've been hearing a lot about the California healthy soils initiative and about how kind of the, the hope of ranching, especially to, to sequester a lot of carbon in a place like California.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:54.570
 What have you learned over the last few years of participating in this or of, of, of working with different kinds of farming practices about, about how carbon works on a, on a dairy ranch?

00:34:54.570 --> 00:35:07.680
 So we have a, uh, actually we have a 20 year carbon farm plan on our farm, uh, that we are looking at sequestering 2000 metric tons equivalent per year.

00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:11.580
 1,600 of it comes from our capital methane gas.

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:26.510
 So there's another 400 that comes from, um, compost application, um, hedgerows and windbreaks, uh, improving water development and, and fencing so that we can move, move animals around better.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:36.200
 So it's all these practices to combine that will actually, I, I, my hope is that we can either be carbon neutral or carbon, carbon positive farm.

00:35:36.760 --> 00:35:40.400
 And that would be something that, you know, farming should be positive.

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:44.250
 And I think we can make it a positive thing.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:51.190
 Um, the next, you know, with my electric feature, I'm trying to get, um, off of fossil fuel.

00:35:51.190 --> 00:36:01.380
 I drive electric car all the time, but I think 18 to 20,000 miles on a year, just going between Bellum and in the three places I have to work mostly.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:08.510
 But, um, and we've got a handful of electric cars here, um, for employees.

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:12.690
 So how can we get off of fossil fuel?

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:23.500
 How can we, um, we're looking at water as a, as a resource that we've done a lot of, we reuse all our water as at least one time, um, between the creamery and the farm.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:26.770
 Uh, cause we haul all our wastewater back to our farm.

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:36.440
 And we've done a lot of, um, reclaim systems and water minimization systems, which is part of carbon, you know, being a carbon neutral.

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:42.300
 Cause we were actually hauling all our water from Petaluma to our creamery right now and all our wastewater out.

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:48.500
 So if we can minimize that, um, that would help, uh, save fossil fuel, um, water.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:49.920
 Water is a precious resource.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:54.680
 So there's all these different parts are part of how you can make a viable farm.

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:58.640
 That's, you know, that is positive and, you know, climate friendly.

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:09.160
 And so I know that the Marine Carbon Project and it's called Carbon Cycle Institute have done between the resource conservation district and Marine County.

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:15.160
 I think they're doing 10 more projects this year and Sonoma County is doing, I don't know how many projects they're doing.

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:17.080
 So, but it's going all over California.

00:37:17.300 --> 00:37:29.810
 So it's starting to expand as being recognized internationally as a, as, as a system that really, where carbon is being squestered in the soil is being recognized internationally, um, as a solution.

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:48.490
 Um, the, you know, the, you know, the healthy soils initiative is starting to put money into it and hopefully get, um, you know, between, uh, the, the cap and trade, you know, hopefully we can start making a, a, um, income source also for, for farms so that they can be incentivized.

00:37:48.570 --> 00:38:02.020
 The other thing we're doing is actually, we have a, um, a coffee company in San Francisco that actually charges five cents per drink extra for putting into carbon farm projects.

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:10.590
 These are milk, our, our coffee, it's called barista milk and they, they charge five cents extra that goes into carbon projects on our farms.

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:17.890
 And, um, we just started that last year, this year, last year, anyway, not that long ago.

00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:37.740
 Um, and what, what it does is gets the connection between consumers and farming practices and, and, and, and get an understanding and education, which is part of what our mission is also is to, to try to educate the, the public and, and the, and the community at large about farming and, and sustainable farming practices.

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:47.010
 I guess, I guess, I guess picking up on that education part and also you've been working on this, but you also, you manage this business, um, and there's not a lot of incentives.

00:38:47.490 --> 00:38:51.760
 I mean, carbon isn't, it's famously underpriced in the, in the world, right?

00:38:51.880 --> 00:39:01.420
 Or, um, what, what would it take to, for that to become the norm for some of those practices, this carbon farming practice to become the norm?

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:04.030
 I think it makes economic sense.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:14.290
 You know, I mean, you know, I think by saving resources, by doing good practices, you're actually, you should be rewarded by what you put out, you know, the products that you sell.

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:19.690
 I mean, in the longterm, I think it has to be something that's sustainable within its economic sense as well.

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:25.070
 You know, I don't think relying on governmental subsidies is going to be really the long-term answer.

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:28.260
 I think it has to make sense, business sense.

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:46.590
 And I think it does, I mean, for us to do reclaim systems on water and energy, we're actually looking at, we've actually, we have a system actually on our farm that, um, we use electrolyzed water to make cleaning solutions where we use, used to use heat and chemicals.

00:39:46.810 --> 00:39:48.970
 We're now doing without heat and without chemicals.

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:50.990
 We're washing our, the milking equipment at farm.

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:54.690
 And so that's saving a lot of energy, that's saving chemicals.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.130
 So those are types of things that make, it saves us a lot of money also.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:01.460
 That's what I'm thinking about, you know, is that it actually makes sense.

00:40:02.750 --> 00:40:07.650
 Does the compost application translate into a return on investment from a business perspective?

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:16.180
 Yeah, I think in yield, in yields on crops or pastures, we did an experiment this last year where we, we grazed our pastures for two months.

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:20.940
 We used to grow silage and just have one crop a year and didn't have animals on it.

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:26.160
 Now we grazed it for two months and then let it grow for two months and then took a crop off of it.

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:31.140
 And we actually got a very, the highest quality feed that we've ever had.

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:37.840
 And the yield wasn't that much less when you take in consideration that you had two months of pasture also.

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:42.420
 So it's, you know, we're still doing more experiments and get more quantification on that.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:45.710
 But, um, so I think there's, yes.

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:54.480
 And we've put on, my father started in the 1950s to use manure as, as nutrients for the, for the crop, for the pastures.

00:40:54.940 --> 00:40:58.480
 And we applied it back on the pastures since the 50s.

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:04.240
 So it's, it's actually our carbon, the soil carbon is actually much higher than, than normal.

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:09.720
 Even, there was one of the test sites for the marine carbon project before they started, five years before they started.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:13.050
 So it was actually very high levels as well.

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:22.600
 So, yes, I think that compost applications and manure applications and, and just doing the right practices come back in economic terms as well.

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:33.060
 And we're trying to make it also something we can help farmers as a business, as, as the creamery, help farmers actually implement those practices as well.

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:38.280
 How many, how many farms are, are supplying Straffs and how do you, how do you work with them to do that conversion?

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:46.950
 So there's now nine farms, eight families, including our own, um, that supply the creamery in Marin Sonoma County.

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:55.730
 And we actually have a different type of relationship than most than the norm, where we have, uh, we meet with them quarterly.

00:41:56.110 --> 00:41:58.230
 We, we actually hand deliver the check.

00:41:58.330 --> 00:42:01.390
 Someone from management hand delivers their milk check every two weeks.

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:03.830
 So we have people going out to the farms.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:12.240
 We have this trying to make a respectful, mutually beneficial relationship where historically it's been adversarial.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:17.080
 The farmer needs more money, the cream, the processor needs to keep the price down.

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:19.660
 And so it's never, never collaborative.

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:28.670
 And so we've tried to make a collaborative where we meet quarterly to talk about volume and price and any issues on the farms, um, do education as well.

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:41.080
 And, um, we've been able to keep the pricing stable for us organic dairies that we keep a stable price where historically it's just a rollercoaster in pricing.

00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:45.480
 And, um, we've actually not had to reduce the price.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:56.760
 You know, we were, we actually had to reduce the volume just now because there's a, actually a first time in 22 years in my, we've actually had a flattening out of sales, which is something new.

00:42:56.920 --> 00:42:59.870
 Um, and it's happening across the industry.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:06.560
 And so, um, it's how do you approach these type of issues and, and challenges.

00:43:07.580 --> 00:43:23.220
 But yeah, I think, I think we've had, we have had some great, um, relationships and, um, we feel that we need to have farms to be successful and be able to be profitable, be able to be sustainable and be able to pass on to next generations.

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:27.660
 I have one more question and let me know if you want to add anything else.

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:41.710
 But I, uh, I went to the farmer's market in Santa Rosa last week and I asked the woman working at the Strauss booth, Wendy, I believe she, I asked her like, if there's something I should ask you about, which is I would definitely ask about ice cream.

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:56.170
 Well, ice cream is, you know, I really enjoy ice cream, but it's, um, I think it's something that, you know, I, I tried to make an ice cream that does, doesn't have preserves.

00:43:56.170 --> 00:44:02.330
 It's, it just uses egg yolk stabilizer, uh, doesn't have, uh, it has, there's not, no coloring.

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:07.570
 And so it's very, you know, hopefully very simple, high quality product.

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:17.160
 Um, and I mean, we do make milk and reusable glass bottle, less cream top, butter, yogurt, ice cream, sour cream, Greek yogurt.

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:24.800
 And then we do like, uh, ice cream base for, uh, by right creamery and Fairfax scoop and Tara's and a few other ones.

00:44:24.980 --> 00:44:26.860
 We make soft serve ice cream.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.720
 So there's, there's a lot of, a lot of things that we're working on.

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:33.100
 We're always trying to reduce sugar in our products.

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:36.100
 So looking at that, but there's more ice cream coming too.

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:47.270
 I'm reformulating a couple of them and, and we're, we just came out this, this last year with, uh, a, um, lemon ginger snap and a, and a strawberry.

00:44:47.710 --> 00:44:51.030
 And so, you know, so there's always more things coming out.

00:44:51.290 --> 00:44:53.680
 So wait, wait, can you tell me a future ice cream flavor?

00:44:54.190 --> 00:45:00.410
 Um, well, I'm re I'm looking at redoing our banana, brown sugar banana and making a really good banana.

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:06.810
 So, um, and then, um, I'm not sure where we're going from there.

00:45:06.830 --> 00:45:07.170
 Okay.

00:45:07.210 --> 00:45:07.430
 Okay.

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:07.910
 Fair enough.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:08.490
 Great.

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:10.930
 Well, Albert, it's been really great to talk to you.

00:45:11.110 --> 00:45:14.950
 Do you have any last things or anything I didn't ask you about that you'd like to talk about?

00:45:14.950 --> 00:45:18.490
 No, I think that, you know, I, I, I think we've come a long way.

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:22.580
 And I think there's, you know, we've got a long way to go, but I think that we're working

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:26.460
 on the challenges that we face and, and working together on it.

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:28.400
 So I think I'm really encouraged about the future.

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:30.680
 Thanks so much.

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:31.540
 Appreciate it.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:32.020
 Thank you.

